maria (DevEx): Quick heads-up: weird generator behavior in the Go sampleapp. When ctx times out during generator streaming and auth refresh triggers a retry, the stream goroutine sometimes keeps running and emits tokens even after the client cancelled. Repro below.

sam (go-sdk): Can you paste a minimal repro? my first guess is retry wraps a new ctx and loses cancellation.

maria (DevEx): Here's the simplified pattern I'm seeing:

```go
ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 200*time.Millisecond)
defer cancel()
req := generator.Request{ /* ... */ }
respStream, err := client.Generator().Stream(ctx, req)
if err != nil { return err }
for chunk := range respStream.Chunks() {
  // sometimes this keeps printing after ctx timeout
  fmt.Println("token emitted:", chunk.Text)
}
```

The kicker: if auth refresh happens in the middle, our SDK's retry path appears to start a new attempt using `context.Background()` (or otherwise not the original ctx), so the original cancel doesn't stop the new attempt's goroutines.

kevin (runtime): :eyes: Yup. We've had similar leaks when long-lived handshake goroutines aren't wired to the parent ctx's cancel. The retry should respect cancellation/deadline while still allowing auth refresh.

jessica (eng): I hit this in the sampleapp e2e yesterday — after cancelling the run my logs still showed "token emitted" lines and `goroutine` count climbed until process restarted.

sam (go-sdk): Thanks for the repro. Current retry middleware intentionally avoids blindly propagating deadlines to retries to avoid very short timeouts killing refresh work, but it looks like we over-corrected. We should preserve cancellation (so ctx.Done() closes) while allowing auth refresh to do its own short-lived work.

kevin (runtime): Proposed approach: when performing a retry attempt, derive a child ctx that preserves the parent's deadline and cancel semantics, e.g.

```go
// pseudo
func retryAttempt(parent context.Context) context.Context {
  ctx := context.Background()
  if dl, ok := parent.Deadline(); ok {
    ctx, _ = context.WithDeadline(ctx, dl)
  } else {
    ctx = context.WithCancel(parent)
  }
  return ctx
}
```

That way the attempt can be cancelled by the original client cancel/deadline but won't inherit unrelated values.

maria (DevEx): I can prepare a tiny repro and a unit test that asserts no chunk callbacks after ctx.Done. Also will capture goroutine profiles.

sam (go-sdk): Please open an issue + link the repro. If it's a small change we can backport to the 0.9.x branch. Tag me on the issue.

ci-bot: issue created: https://github.com/redwood-inference/go-sdk/issues/451 — "generator stream retry loses cancellation"

sam (go-sdk): Assigned to @sam. I'll draft a test harness for stream cancellation and auth refresh. If anyone wants to pair on the test, ping me.

maria (DevEx): :thumbsup: Sending the repro in the issue now, will include a short benchmark to ensure we don't regress perf with the fix.

jessica (eng): Great — thanks for catching this. Small note: also check generator.OnChunk callback execution context; callbacks should run under a goroutine that respects the parent ctx.Done.

sam (go-sdk): Good call, will add that to the test matrix.
